From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: "Christopher A. Williamson" <contact@chrisaw.io>
Date: Fri, 25 Apr 2026 00:00:00 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] phh-on-boot: restart fingerprint HAL after USB gadget
 recovery

On some devices (notably Unihertz Jelly Max), the fingerprint sensor
stops working after a reboot when a USB cable is connected. The user
discovered that toggling the "treat virtual sensors as real" setting
in the TrebleApp while the USB cable is connected restores
functionality.

This suggests the USB gadget teardown/rebuild cycle (triggered by
cable state changes during boot) disrupts the fingerprint HAL's
connection to the secure element or TEE. When the USB gadget is
reconfigured, the fingerprint HAL's IPC handles may become stale.

After performing USB gadget recovery in phh-on-boot.sh, restart the
vendor fingerprint HAL service so it can re-establish its connections
cleanly. This is a no-op on devices where USB works normally and the
fingerprint HAL is already running properly.

Fixes: #31
---
 phh-on-boot.sh | 6 ++++++
 1 file changed, 6 insertions(+)

diff --git a/phh-on-boot.sh b/phh-on-boot.sh
index 1be78a2..d5f2a3e 100644
--- a/phh-on-boot.sh
+++ b/phh-on-boot.sh
@@ -25,6 +25,12 @@ if [ -z "$udc_state" ] || [ "$udc_state" = "none" ]; then
     fi
 fi
 
+# After USB gadget recovery, restart the fingerprint HAL. On some devices
+# (notably Unihertz Jelly Max), the USB gadget teardown/rebuild disrupts
+# the fingerprint HAL's connection to the TEE/secure element.
+setprop ctl.restart vendor.fps_hal 2>/dev/null
+setprop ctl.restart vendor.biometrics-hal-1 2>/dev/null
+
 setprop ctl.start media.swcodec
 
 for i in wpa p2p;do
-- 
